A1. This is very misleading. ~ DECOY
A6. There's nothing fictitious about these. ~ FACTS
A11. A sheik's work-bench. ~ DIVAN
A12. This is sometimes tried on night-clubs—but it hardly ever fits. ~ LID
A14. This is often a dangerous curve. ~ SHAPE
A16. No end, no end! ~ EVER
A17. Sweetest thing you ever saw! ~ SYRUP
A19. The last word in prayers. ~ AMEN
A20. All fighters have one, so do a lot of married men. ~ MANAGER
A21. She'll forgive your past—for this. ~ PRESENT
A22. Just Mollie and me—without the offspring. ~ US
A23. The bitter half of a matrimonial mess. ~ WIFEY
A25. With some practice this can get along. (Abbr.) ~ DR
A26. You'll never get far by putting your shoulder to this. ~ MAT
A28. A big fish from Newfoundland. ~ COD
A29. What is cluttered with political propaganda now? ~ AIR
A31. This is a bracer—if you're sick. ~ TONIC
A33. A literary composition. ~ ESSAY
A35. The funny Irishman of the Comics. ~ PAT
A36. In what Horizontal 11 is found. (Plural.) ~ PARLORS
A37. A bear's boudoir. ~ DEN
A38. A perch. ~ ROOST
A40. Fine stuff for a bow. ~ RESIN
A43. Kinds of large sea-fish allied to the shark. ~ RAY
A44. What clinging vines cling to. ~ MEN
A46. This is less than a crowd. ~ TWO
A48. Part of the verb to be. ~ IS
A50. Bloomers. ~ ROSES
A52. Behold! ~ LO
A53. This kind of billet-doux will get somewhere. ~ STAMPED
A55. What the Prodigal Son was. ~ REVELER
A57. Unadulterated. ~ PURE
A58. You must dig if you're going to do this. ~ DELVE
A60. These kind of people are bound to have trouble. ~ TIED
A61. A low-down, crafty person. ~ SNEAK
A63. Short lyric or narrative poem. ~ LAY
A64. What the little chorus darlings are to Reggie. ~ DEARS
A65. An artist wouldn't mind being hung here. ~ SALON
A66. These are the cat's. ~ PURRS

D1. What chorus girls would like to be. ~ DIVAS
D2. A poetic evening. ~ EVEN
D3. This goes a long way in a dry country. ~ CARAVAN
D4. Where Horizontal 12 is put but seldom kept. ~ ON
D5. This usually precedes "How dare you!" ~ SIR
D7. In the same degree. ~ AS
D8. The nude body—of a car. ~ CHASSIS
D9. You'll never find a wild-cat this way. ~ TAME
D10. The author of Annie Laurie might lie him doon an dee for her; but we'll bet he wouldn't do this. ~ SPEND
D11. What flappers never do when invited to dine. ~ DEMUR
D12. This is poetic. ~ LYRIC
D13. Hornswoggled, deceived—or what were you? ~ DUPED
D15. This will let you in. ~ ENTRY
D17. What doughboys have to do after a tear. ~ SEW
D18. What reformers love to do. ~ PRY
D24. There's a lot of kickers in these. ~ FOLLIES
D26. A bad place for a miss. ~ MOTOR
D27. This kind of person is tight even if he isn't Scotch. ~ TIPSY
D29. This can't be a liability. ~ ASSET
D30. This brings things home to you. ~ RADIO
D31. An old salt. ~ TAR
D32. The Pied Piper's rival. ~ CAT
D33. It's wrong to do this. ~ ERR
D34. A desire or a piece of Chinese cash. ~ YEN
D39. Good old-fashioned breakfast food. ~ OATMEAL
D41. What a little more Horizontal 17 will make the griddle cakes. ~ SWEETER
D42. One does this who speaks imperfectly. ~ LISPS
D44. Even the reformers can't get anything on this. ~ MODEL
D45. The kind of people who take trans-Atlantic flights. ~ NERVY
D47. The weapons used in political contests. ~ WORDS
D49. What a blackjack does. ~ STUNS
D50. A stop signal. ~ RED
D51. You must have vision to do this. ~ SEE
D52. What the stage villain does. ~ LEERS
D54. Superficial extent. ~ AREA
D56. There's something false about this. ~ LIAR
D59. A profession at which V. 56 should be successful. ~ LAW
D62. The aim of two-fisted young men. (Abbr.) ~ KO
D64. A French preposition. ~ DU


